<h1>Extra Setting Stuff</h1>
<ul>
<li><p>even among the supernatural, most people are not aware of the true power of belief. Only the especially old and powerful can directly change the world through their own willpower, though the many forms of lesser magic and supernatural abilities can be said to be an implicit form of the same power.</p>
</li>
<li><p>'Awakening' an adult human so that they can safely view the supernatural is a slow and dangerous process for everyone involved. It is much easier (though still dangerous) to draw belief from children children.</p>
</li>
<li><p>trolls gained great power through the rise of the internet. Turns out weblinks are symbolically much like bridges.</p>
</li>
<li><p>well loved toys are often given life, but they almost always de-animate as the child grows up. They cannot act in front of all but the very youngest of children.</p>
</li>
<li><p>the island of lost toys is a real place. The toys there are not only sustained the lingering belief of thier child, but also the longing of all people for precious things lost.</p>
</li>
<li><p>there are a few groups with the goal of breaking past the 'Law of Normalcy' by awakening enough humans into true belief of the supernatural. It is their belief that this law didn't always exist and is perhaps quite a bit newer than you would think. This is considered a radical idea by the average supernatural. For some reason they never seem to get very far with their plans, even locally.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The reality bending part of the setting is inspired by the World of Darkness game, Mage: the Ascension.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Animated children's television show characters are often real and quite powerful. A lot of them work in hollywood. For some reason live action characters rarely become real.</p>
</li>
<li><p>'Santa Claus' is title inherited by an awakened human. (<a href="https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0111070/">The Santa Clause 1994</a>)</p>
</li>
</ul>

